<speak>
<p>We want to divide h of one, P, h of negative one, P, and h of i, P by two, while keeping integer or Gaussian integer coefficients. It is enough to check one power, P to the b, at a time. Select that same position in the four sign polynomials. Call its coefficients a zero, a one, a two, and a three. Each is positive or negative one, so each is odd. Keep the power P to the b fixed while we calculate with its four coefficients.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>At Z equal to one, the coefficient is the sum of all four signs. At Z equal to negative one, the signs on a one and a three are reversed. Work modulo two: both positive and negative versions of an odd integer have residue one. Each expression therefore becomes four ones. Pair the ones: each pair has even sum, and the total has residue zero.<break time="700ms"/>Return to the original integer expressions. Both are even, so dividing either by two gives an integer. We have justified the two real half coefficients.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>At Z equal to i, separate the coefficient into a zero minus a two, plus i times the difference of a one and a three. Each difference of two signs is even. Divide the real difference and the imaginary difference separately by two. Both halves are integers, so their combination is a Gaussian integer.<break time="700ms"/>For one coefficient check, choose the column negative one, one, one, negative one. The first real half is zero. The second real half is also zero. The Gaussian half is negative one plus i. This is one column illustrating parity, not a constructed Hadamard row.<break time="700ms"/>Repeat the same argument independently at every power of P. Therefore the first two polynomials have integer half coefficients, and the third has Gaussian integer half coefficients. The proof uses only the common signs. No norm identity is needed.<break time="1100ms"/></p>
</speak>
